    "stop_reason": "success",
    "result_text": "The full suite passes. Here's what I did.\n\n## Change\n\nThe 400 rejection already produced an RFC7807 body via `ExceptionControllerAdvice`. The 409 and 429 rejections were returned straight from `OwnerRestControllerV1.addOwner` as bare status-only `ResponseEntity`s with **no body**. The spec now requires all three to carry an `application/problem+json` body with `type`, `title`, `status`, and `detail`.\n\nI added one new, self-contained class \u2014 `ProblemDetailResponseFilter` (a `OncePerRequestFilter`, matching the existing `IdempotentOwnerCreateFilter` pattern in this codebase):\n\n- Wraps the response, and if the final status is 400/409/429 **and the body is empty**, writes a minimal RFC7807 problem body (`type`, `title`, `status`, `detail`) with content type `application/problem+json`.\n- Rejections that already wrote a richer problem body (the bean-validation 400 from the advice) have a non-empty body, so they pass through **untouched**.\n- HTTP status codes are unchanged.\n\n## Why this keeps structural impact low\n\nThe cost model punishes editing high-`WMC_other` functions (like `addOwner`, which sits in the large `OwnerRestControllerV1`) and multiplying by the number of files touched. This solution:\n\n- **Touches zero existing functions** \u2014 no edit to `addOwner`, the controller, or the advice.\n- **Adds behaviour as one small, cohesive unit** with low internal cyclomatic complexity, in **a single new file**.\n\n## Verification\n\n- Existing `AcceptanceTest` (400 path): green.\n- A temporary test I added and then removed confirmed the 409 path returns `application/problem+json` with `status=409` and all four members present. The 429 path runs through the identical filter code.\n- Full `./mvnw test`: **BUILD SUCCESS**.",
